Beast in a Labyrinth

A beast is in a labyrinth. The labyrinth is represented as a matrix of individual positions. At any moment, the beast is at one particular position and is turned in one of four possible directions (up, down, left and right).

In each round the beast makes one move. The possible moves are: TurnLeft, TurnRight, MakeStep (one step forward). At the beginning, the beast will always have a wall to its right. As the beast moves it tries to follow this wall (see the example below).

Read input from the file maze.txt. The input contains a width and height on separate lines, followed by a map of the labyrinth. Individual characters depict individual positions in the labyrinth: 'X' is a wall and '.' is an empty spot. The characters '^', '>', 'v' and '<' depict the beast turned upward, to the right, downward and to the left, respectively.

Your program should read the input and then move the beast 20 times. After each move, print a map of the labyrinth in the same form in which you read it. Write an empty line after each map.

Beware: The first output is performed after the first move of the beast.

Don't forget to look at the hints that appear after the long sample output below.

